1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,489
The first of the month, and the direct deposit lands in the account.

2
00:00:04,289 --> 00:00:07,550
For about two seconds that notification feels like relief,

3
00:00:07,550 --> 00:00:10,924
and three old Japanese methods are what make it last longer.

4
00:00:11,724 --> 00:00:14,210
Then rent goes out. Then the electric bill,

5
00:00:14,210 --> 00:00:16,870
which is somehow higher than it was last year.

6
00:00:17,670 --> 00:00:19,450
Then the car insurance renewal,

7
00:00:19,450 --> 00:00:23,182
the one you forgot bills every six months instead of every month.

8
00:00:23,982 --> 00:00:26,576
A birthday gift, two food delivery orders,

9
00:00:26,576 --> 00:00:29,912
and one streaming service that quietly renewed itself.

10
00:00:30,712 --> 00:00:34,457
Nothing on that list was big. Every one of them was small,

11
00:00:34,457 --> 00:00:36,459
and every one of them was fair.

12
00:00:37,259 --> 00:00:40,838
And by the twentieth the balance looks like the paycheck never arrived.

13
00:00:41,638 --> 00:00:44,006
So you scroll back through the statement,

14
00:00:44,006 --> 00:00:46,836
looking for the one big expense that explains it.

15
00:00:47,636 --> 00:00:50,687
It is not in there. The money did not go to one place,

16
00:00:50,687 --> 00:00:52,495
it went a little bit everywhere.

17
00:00:53,295 --> 00:00:56,639
If that is your month, every month, then this one is for you.

18
00:00:57,439 --> 00:01:00,903
Today, three Japanese money methods that are centuries old,

19
00:01:00,903 --> 00:01:03,839
and that are taught in no finance course anywhere.

20
00:01:04,639 --> 00:01:08,099
But first, the number that put Japan on your feed in the first place,

21
00:01:08,099 --> 00:01:10,255
because that number is doing a lot of work.

22
00:01:11,055 --> 00:01:13,517
The internet says a regular Japanese household

23
00:01:13,517 --> 00:01:16,567
saves thirty-seven percent of its pay every single month.

24
00:01:17,367 --> 00:01:22,226
That number is real. It is not made up, and Japan's own government publishes it.

25
00:01:23,026 --> 00:01:26,527
In the twenty twenty-four Family Income and Expenditure Survey,

26
00:01:26,527 --> 00:01:29,139
the number is thirty-seven point eight percent.

27
00:01:29,938 --> 00:01:32,323
The same year, the same government's national

28
00:01:32,323 --> 00:01:35,502
accounts put the household saving rate at about one percent.

29
00:01:36,302 --> 00:01:39,590
One country. One year. One government. And a gap

30
00:01:39,590 --> 00:01:43,015
between the two figures of more than thirty times.

31
00:01:43,816 --> 00:01:46,603
The difference is not Japanese discipline,

32
00:01:46,603 --> 00:01:50,320
and it is not a secret. It is who gets counted, and how.

33
00:01:51,120 --> 00:01:55,803
The thirty-seven counts salaried households only. The other one counts everybody,

34
00:01:55,803 --> 00:01:58,173
including retirees and the self-employed.

35
00:01:58,973 --> 00:02:01,532
Both numbers are true. Only the big one travels,

36
00:02:01,532 --> 00:02:04,250
because the small one does not sell on a thumbnail.

37
00:02:05,050 --> 00:02:08,768
So the real question is what actually survives inside these methods,

38
00:02:08,768 --> 00:02:11,502
and what it does to four thousand dollars a month.

39
00:02:12,302 --> 00:02:16,317
That same survey also records where the monthly surplus actually goes,

40
00:02:16,317 --> 00:02:18,153
which is the part nobody quotes.

41
00:02:18,954 --> 00:02:21,774
About ninety percent of it goes straight into bank

42
00:02:21,774 --> 00:02:24,988
deposits. About three percent goes into stocks and bonds.

43
00:02:25,788 --> 00:02:29,983
So Japan saves hard, and then puts almost none of that saving to work,

44
00:02:29,983 --> 00:02:31,901
which is a very different thing.

45
00:02:32,701 --> 00:02:35,598
One Bank of Japan table says fifty-one percent of

46
00:02:35,598 --> 00:02:39,205
Japanese household financial assets sit in cash and deposits.

47
00:02:40,005 --> 00:02:43,481
The next column of the same table puts American households at

48
00:02:43,481 --> 00:02:47,241
eleven point five percent in cash and forty-one percent in stocks.

49
00:02:48,041 --> 00:02:52,949
That is the half of the story most videos skip. Japan is an example of saving,

50
00:02:52,949 --> 00:02:54,963
and not an example of investing.

51
00:02:55,763 --> 00:02:57,918
And here is the part almost nobody

52
00:02:57,918 --> 00:03:01,088
mentions. Japan was not always a nation of savers,

53
00:03:01,088 --> 00:03:02,529
and the timing matters.

54
00:03:03,329 --> 00:03:07,579
After the war, the rate was above fifteen percent for exactly twenty-five years,

55
00:03:07,579 --> 00:03:09,598
from nineteen sixty-one to eighty-six.

56
00:03:10,399 --> 00:03:13,253
Its postwar peak was near twenty-three percent,

57
00:03:13,253 --> 00:03:17,504
and since two thousand two it has hardly ever been above five percent.

58
00:03:18,304 --> 00:03:22,303
The largest study of that history says the cause was not culture,

59
00:03:22,303 --> 00:03:24,887
not tradition, and not national character.

60
00:03:25,687 --> 00:03:28,824
The causes were no consumer credit, no safety net,

61
00:03:28,824 --> 00:03:33,027
fast income growth, a tax break, and a government savings campaign.

62
00:03:33,827 --> 00:03:36,696
So the methods work. They were simply never the

63
00:03:36,696 --> 00:03:40,175
reason. That is the most honest line in this whole video.

64
00:03:40,975 --> 00:03:43,814
Method one is mottainai. Roughly translated,

65
00:03:43,814 --> 00:03:47,427
it means the real value of a thing quietly going unused.

66
00:03:48,227 --> 00:03:51,428
It is not a lecture about waste. It is regret that

67
00:03:51,428 --> 00:03:54,758
something you already paid for was never fully used.

68
00:03:55,558 --> 00:03:59,091
The shirt hanging in your closet holds a farmer's crop,

69
00:03:59,091 --> 00:04:02,559
a mill's hours, a truck's diesel, and a store's shelf.

70
00:04:03,359 --> 00:04:07,800
It also holds the hours of your own working life, the ones you traded to pay for it.

71
00:04:08,599 --> 00:04:11,055
Pause this video. Stand up, open your closet,

72
00:04:11,055 --> 00:04:14,111
and count the clothes that are still wearing their tags.

73
00:04:14,911 --> 00:04:17,904
Then open your phone, go to the subscriptions screen,

74
00:04:17,904 --> 00:04:21,180
and read the whole list of things billing you every month.

75
00:04:21,981 --> 00:04:24,160
Then open the refrigerator and look at the food

76
00:04:24,160 --> 00:04:26,709
that is going to be thrown out by the end of next week.

77
00:04:27,509 --> 00:04:30,156
None of that is a shortage of money. All three of

78
00:04:30,156 --> 00:04:33,073
them are value you already bought and then never used.

79
00:04:33,873 --> 00:04:36,277
Mottainai collapses into a single question,

80
00:04:36,277 --> 00:04:39,855
and the question gets asked before the purchase, never after it.

81
00:04:40,655 --> 00:04:44,049
Will its full value actually get used? If the answer is maybe,

82
00:04:44,049 --> 00:04:45,253
then the answer is no.

83
00:04:46,052 --> 00:04:50,318
On four thousand dollars a month that question comes up maybe three or four times,

84
00:04:50,318 --> 00:04:52,139
and every time it stops a purchase.

85
00:04:52,939 --> 00:04:57,071
That is the whole job of mottainai. It is not about spending less,

86
00:04:57,071 --> 00:04:59,888
it is about using up what was already bought.

87
00:05:00,688 --> 00:05:05,129
The next method starts at the dinner table, and it ends inside your bank account.

88
00:05:05,928 --> 00:05:10,462
Method two is hara hachi bu, an old Okinawan habit with Confucian roots,

89
00:05:10,462 --> 00:05:13,295
and it has nothing to do with money at first.

90
00:05:14,095 --> 00:05:18,227
It means you stop eating when your stomach is eight parts full out of ten,

91
00:05:18,227 --> 00:05:19,790
not when the plate is empty.

92
00:05:20,590 --> 00:05:21,863
Fullness arrives late,

93
00:05:21,863 --> 00:05:25,971
so the last two parts are always more than the body actually asked for.

94
00:05:26,771 --> 00:05:30,361
Now put that same rule on a paycheck. Live on eight parts,

95
00:05:30,361 --> 00:05:32,466
and move two parts out on day one.

96
00:05:33,266 --> 00:05:37,411
On four thousand dollars a month, two parts is eight hundred dollars moved out,

97
00:05:37,411 --> 00:05:39,614
and thirty-two hundred dollars to live on.

98
00:05:40,413 --> 00:05:43,505
And it happens the day after payday, not on the thirtieth,

99
00:05:43,505 --> 00:05:46,917
because whatever is left at the end of the month is always zero.

100
00:05:47,718 --> 00:05:49,965
Automate it so it moves without a decision,

101
00:05:49,965 --> 00:05:53,883
into an account that is separate from the one the card and the bills touch.

102
00:05:54,683 --> 00:05:58,209
This is not discipline, and it is not about willpower. It is

103
00:05:58,209 --> 00:06:01,971
one decision on day one that carries the other twenty-nine days.

104
00:06:02,771 --> 00:06:04,078
In Japan's own survey,

105
00:06:04,078 --> 00:06:08,355
salaried households spend only sixty-two percent of their take-home pay,

106
00:06:08,355 --> 00:06:10,059
and the rest is that surplus.

107
00:06:10,859 --> 00:06:14,468
If twenty percent is too much right now, start at five percent,

108
00:06:14,468 --> 00:06:17,390
which on that same paycheck is two hundred dollars.

109
00:06:18,190 --> 00:06:22,446
And here is the honest part. If the bills already eat the whole paycheck,

110
00:06:22,446 --> 00:06:25,478
this fails in month one, and that is not your fault.

111
00:06:26,278 --> 00:06:29,015
Then the problem is not saving, it is accounting,

112
00:06:29,015 --> 00:06:33,148
and the accounting method was written by a woman over a hundred years ago.

113
00:06:33,948 --> 00:06:37,829
Method three is kakeibo. It translates as household account book,

114
00:06:37,829 --> 00:06:40,217
and it is written by hand, once a month.

115
00:06:41,017 --> 00:06:44,246
It was created in nineteen oh four by Hani Motoko,

116
00:06:44,246 --> 00:06:46,764
who was Japan's first woman journalist.

117
00:06:47,564 --> 00:06:50,560
It has been printed every single year since then,

118
00:06:50,560 --> 00:06:52,945
with four years missing during the war.

119
00:06:53,746 --> 00:06:56,252
Her actual invention was not a four column

120
00:06:56,252 --> 00:06:59,415
chart. Her actual invention was the idea of a budget.

121
00:07:00,214 --> 00:07:03,921
Divide the year's income by twelve, set the saving aside first,

122
00:07:03,921 --> 00:07:06,980
and then split whatever is left across the expenses.

123
00:07:07,780 --> 00:07:10,481
In nineteen oh four that was a new idea.

124
00:07:10,481 --> 00:07:14,128
Saving gets decided before the spending, not after it.

125
00:07:14,928 --> 00:07:18,219
The four categories everybody teaches today, needs,

126
00:07:18,219 --> 00:07:21,380
wants, culture, and unexpected, came a lot later.

127
00:07:22,180 --> 00:07:25,202
No Japanese source attributes them to Hani Motoko,

128
00:07:25,202 --> 00:07:28,345
and her own ledger used a different set of headings.

129
00:07:29,145 --> 00:07:31,817
What actually survives is four questions,

130
00:07:31,817 --> 00:07:35,597
and they get asked at the start of the month, not the end.

131
00:07:36,397 --> 00:07:39,503
How much came in. How much gets set aside. How

132
00:07:39,503 --> 00:07:42,745
much goes out. And what changes from last month.

133
00:07:43,545 --> 00:07:47,014
Notice that question two comes before question three,

134
00:07:47,014 --> 00:07:49,632
because that order is the entire method.

135
00:07:50,431 --> 00:07:53,306
It happens on paper. A pen, a notebook,

136
00:07:53,306 --> 00:07:57,066
the phone face down, fifteen minutes, once a month.

137
00:07:57,866 --> 00:08:00,446
An app reports where the money went. A paper

138
00:08:00,446 --> 00:08:03,143
ledger decides where the money is going to go.

139
00:08:03,943 --> 00:08:07,013
It decides whether a four hundred dollar surprise is an

140
00:08:07,013 --> 00:08:10,474
inconvenience or a card balance at over twenty percent a year.

141
00:08:11,274 --> 00:08:13,275
In the latest Federal Reserve survey,

142
00:08:13,275 --> 00:08:17,439
about four in ten adults could not cover that four hundred dollars with cash.

143
00:08:18,239 --> 00:08:21,743
The promise at the top was three methods. There is a fourth one,

144
00:08:21,743 --> 00:08:23,385
and it is the hardest of them.

145
00:08:24,185 --> 00:08:27,009
At Ryoan-ji temple in Kyoto there is a stone water

146
00:08:27,009 --> 00:08:30,454
basin with four characters cut around the hole in its center.

147
00:08:31,254 --> 00:08:33,831
Together they say, what there is, is enough,

148
00:08:33,831 --> 00:08:36,818
and knowing only that is the whole of the teaching.

149
00:08:37,618 --> 00:08:40,171
All four characters share one single part,

150
00:08:40,171 --> 00:08:44,122
and that shared part is the emptiness at the centre of the basin.

151
00:08:44,923 --> 00:08:47,894
On its own, not one of them is a complete character.

152
00:08:47,894 --> 00:08:51,323
Each of them needs that emptiness in the middle to be whole.

153
00:08:52,123 --> 00:08:56,188
Now look at your own last ten years. The degree, the first job,

154
00:08:56,188 --> 00:08:59,672
the first paycheck, the phone, the car, the apartment.

155
00:09:00,472 --> 00:09:02,521
Every time it felt like the finish line,

156
00:09:02,521 --> 00:09:05,749
and every time the line moved a few weeks after you crossed it.

157
00:09:06,549 --> 00:09:10,338
Income went up, and the definition of a need went up right behind it,

158
00:09:10,338 --> 00:09:12,479
which is why the raise never showed up.

159
00:09:13,279 --> 00:09:15,891
An entire industry runs on making whatever you

160
00:09:15,891 --> 00:09:18,843
already own feel like less than whatever comes next.

161
00:09:19,643 --> 00:09:21,603
Taru wo shiru asks one question,

162
00:09:21,603 --> 00:09:26,748
and it is the only one here you have to answer for yourself. What is enough for you?

163
00:09:27,548 --> 00:09:31,519
Do not answer that quickly, because three methods tell you how to keep money,

164
00:09:31,519 --> 00:09:33,582
and the fourth tells you what it is for.

165
00:09:34,382 --> 00:09:36,183
Mottainai. Before the purchase,

166
00:09:36,183 --> 00:09:39,842
ask whether the full value of the thing will actually get used.

167
00:09:40,642 --> 00:09:43,706
Hara hachi bu. Move two parts out the day after payday,

168
00:09:43,706 --> 00:09:46,102
automatically, and live on the other eight.

169
00:09:46,901 --> 00:09:50,613
Kakeibo. Write the four questions on paper at the start of the month,

170
00:09:50,613 --> 00:09:53,249
and fill in the saving line before anything else.

171
00:09:54,049 --> 00:09:56,766
Taru wo shiru. Decide what is enough for you,

172
00:09:56,766 --> 00:09:59,482
or the other three methods do nothing at all.

173
00:10:00,283 --> 00:10:02,553
Tonight, one sheet of paper, one pen,

174
00:10:02,553 --> 00:10:06,970
and those four questions. Fifteen minutes, and it is done for the month.

175
00:10:07,770 --> 00:10:10,652
What you heard today is information. What you start

176
00:10:10,652 --> 00:10:13,648
tonight is the only part of it that changes anything.

177
00:10:14,448 --> 00:10:16,365
Comment which of the four hit hardest,

178
00:10:16,365 --> 00:10:20,299
and subscribe if you want money explained this plainly, without a sales pitch.

179
00:10:21,099 --> 00:10:23,328
And send this to the friend who says every

180
00:10:23,328 --> 00:10:26,193
single month that they have no idea where it all went.
